          "id": "sec-1-3",
          "secNumber": "1.3",
          "heading": "Unit engineering case \u2014 Warehouse roof reactions",
          "content": "### Unit engineering case \u2014 Warehouse roof reactions\n\nA warehouse roof has a redundant bearing installed after a temporary stage. Determine the bearing load under dead load and under a subsequent live load, taking the actual support displacement to be zero. Begin with a released-beam cantilever, determine the unrestrained tip displacement and apply a compatibility correction. A useful sensitivity question is whether a soft bearing of finite stiffness meaningfully changes peak reaction. To avoid disguising uncertainty, compare an ideal rigid prop with a spring bearing and state why the result is conditional on bearing stiffness.\n\n#### Investigation sequence\n\nBegin by preparing a diagram that names every member, support, load, imposed movement and unknown. Choose an independent coordinate system and assign a sign to every generalized force. Derive a minimal reference calculation before using a computational model. Then run a sensitivity study in which only one physical assumption changes at a time. Record both the predicted movement and the reaction or internal-force counterpart. Check conservation of force and moment where the physical model supports those checks. Finally compare the calculation with a plausible measurement or with a more complete independent model. Label the evidence as a verification benchmark, an assumption or a genuinely observed fact.\n\n#### Suggested written report\n\nA useful report has a one-paragraph engineering question, a table of parameter values with units, an explicit statement of boundary conditions, the governing equations, a readable figure of the system, a table of numerical results, at least one independent verification, and a conclusion that identifies unverified assumptions. State what happens when the governing input doubles, when stiffness increases, and when a support is relaxed or stiffened. Explain whether the observed changes follow from algebraic scaling or from a change in load path. Keep the original units in the file even if a graph displays a converted unit."
