          "heading": "Compatibility coordinates",
          "content": "### 1.2 Compatibility coordinates\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nIdentify a generalized displacement paired with each redundant reaction. Translational displacement and rotation are different kinematic quantities and need separate sign conventions.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**\u0394_i^L + \u03a3_j f_ij X_j = \u0394_i^prescribed.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nFor a redundant vertical prop reaction R, the released-beam tip displacement from real loading plus the displacement induced by R must equal the actual prop displacement, often zero.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nCalling the released-beam deflection zero before including the redundant silently discards the reason the force method is needed.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nApply one redundant at a time, confirm deflection directions, then substitute all solved redundants to calculate residual compatibility.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nAssess the effect of construction-stage propping before deck concrete reaches strength. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Calling the released-beam deflection zero before including the redundant silently discards the reason the force method is needed. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Assess the effect of construction-stage propping before deck concrete reaches strength.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
