          "heading": "Unit-load flexibility",
          "content": "### 1.3 Unit-load flexibility\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nThe principle of virtual work derives flexibility as an integral of products of internal force diagrams for real and unit generalized loads. For slender beams, bending often dominates; for trusses axial deformation is central.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**f_ij = \u222b(M_i M_j/EI)dx + \u222b(N_i N_j/EA)dx; omit terms only with a justified approximation.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nA simply supported prismatic beam of length L with a unit central force has a midspan displacement coefficient f = L\u00b3/(48EI). Under force P, \u03b4 = P f.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nA unit moment produces a rotation, not a translational displacement, so using a force-load formula for a rotational coordinate is dimensionally invalid.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nRecalculate the coefficient by independently applying the real load to the released system and verifying force\u2013displacement reciprocity f_ij = f_ji.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nEstimate how a strengthening plate changes a deflection-sensitive steel floor joist. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: A unit moment produces a rotation, not a translational displacement, so using a force-load formula for a rotational coordinate is dimensionally invalid. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Estimate how a strengthening plate changes a deflection-sensitive steel floor joist.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
