          "heading": "Conceptual overview",
          "content": "### Conceptual overview\n\nA displacement method chooses rotations and translations as unknowns and computes restoring member forces from these movements. Instead of releasing redundants, one imagines restraints added to prevent joint movement and then releases them by satisfying force and moment equilibrium. Slope\u2013deflection, moment distribution and matrix stiffness all express the same linear mechanics using different bookkeeping. In a non-sway continuous beam, support vertical displacement may be prescribed, so joint rotations become the principal unknowns. In a sway frame, lateral translations must also be retained. The stiffness coefficient for an Euler\u2013Bernoulli prismatic member is dependent on its far-end condition; a far-end fixed member exhibits rotational stiffness 4EI/L, while a far-end hinged member exhibits 3EI/L. These coefficients do not apply to a short deep beam when shear flexibility is significant."
