          "content": "### 3.2 Global assembly and constraints\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nScatter every element stiffness contribution into the global matrix at indices corresponding to connected nodal DOFs. Enforce prescribed displacements and solve only for unknown DOFs.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**K_ff u_f = F_f \u2212 K_fc u_c; R_c = K_cf u_f + K_cc u_c \u2212 F_c.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nA two-bay frame may have 18 free and restrained DOFs before simplifying symmetry; indexing is the primary practical risk in manual assembly.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nDeleting a support row without deleting the matching column breaks symmetry; setting a diagonal entry to a giant number can obscure reaction recovery.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nReinsert the solved displacement vector into the original complete system and check the constrained residual components as support reactions.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nDiscuss why settlement and imposed temperature strain must enter the same equilibrium bookkeeping. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Deleting a support row without deleting the matching column breaks symmetry; setting a diagonal entry to a giant number can obscure reaction recovery. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Discuss why settlement and imposed temperature strain must enter the same equilibrium bookkeeping.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
