          "content": "### 3.3 Three-bar truss numerical model\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nA top joint joined to three fixed lower joints produces a two-unknown stiffness system. Different bar lengths and orientations contribute different off-diagonal coupling and effective vertical stiffness.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**For top displacements (ux,uy), K = \u03a3(EA/L)[[cx\u00b2,cxcy],[cxcy,cy\u00b2]] and bar force N_i=(EA/L)(cx ux+cy uy).**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nFor a symmetric support arrangement and vertical force, ux is zero by symmetry, but the center vertical member still changes the partition of axial force.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nAdding a bar while reusing forces from the former two-bar solution violates equilibrium and compatibility: the new stiffness must be assembled and solved again.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nCheck horizontal and vertical nodal equilibrium from recovered bar forces and document the chosen sign for tension.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nUse Lab 05 to measure how a vertical hanger relieves two diagonal members. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Adding a bar while reusing forces from the former two-bar solution violates equilibrium and compatibility: the new stiffness must be assembled and solved again. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Use Lab 05 to measure how a vertical hanger relieves two diagonal members.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
