          "content": "### 3.4 Plane-frame local stiffness\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nThe plane-frame element couples two translational DOFs and one rotational DOF at each end; its local stiffness is the sum of axial EA/L and Euler\u2013Bernoulli bending terms.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**Local bending terms: 12EI/L\u00b3, 6EI/L\u00b2, 4EI/L and 2EI/L; axial term EA/L.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nFor a prismatic 6 m steel beam with EI=30 MN\u00b7m\u00b2, its local rotational diagonal is 4EI/L=20 MN\u00b7m/rad.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nA released member end is not the same as a rigid joint with small EI. To model an actual hinge, release the member rotational DOF correctly.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nCheck that a member oriented vertically produces the same physical stiffness after correct transformation as a rotated horizontal equivalent.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nCompare a sway portal and a non-sway portal under the same horizontal action. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: A released member end is not the same as a rigid joint with small EI. To model an actual hinge, release the member rotational DOF correctly. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Compare a sway portal and a non-sway portal under the same horizontal action.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
