          "content": "### 5.5 Model applicability and design checks\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nSecond-order analyses require trustworthy support stiffness, geometric imperfections, gravity combinations and material stiffness. Verification should distinguish calculated elastic critical loads from design strengths.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**A stability index can be formed from gravity effects and drift, but its precise acceptance threshold is defined by the applicable design code and frame classification.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nA structure can meet a drift target in linear analysis yet fail a strength or buckling check when axial force amplifies moments.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nTreating an educational idealization as a certified safety check is unsafe, particularly for high-rise structures, bridges, shoring and occupied buildings.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nRun a sensitivity table on stiffness, restraint and load combination; document governing assumptions rather than reporting a single unqualified reserve ratio.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nDevelop a checklist for independent review of a contractor-designed shoring tower. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Treating an educational idealization as a certified safety check is unsafe, particularly for high-rise structures, bridges, shoring and occupied buildings. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Develop a checklist for independent review of a contractor-designed shoring tower.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
