          "heading": "Reaction influence lines",
          "content": "### 6.1 Reaction influence lines\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nFor a single span with two simple supports, a unit moving vertical load produces end reactions proportional to distance from the opposite support. The response changes continuously along the span.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**RA(x)=(L\u2212x)/L and RB(x)=x/L for unit downward load at 0\u2264x\u2264L.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nOn a 20 m span a unit load at x=7 m creates reactions RA=0.65 and RB=0.35 in consistent force units.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nThe ordinate is a reaction per unit moving load, not a real reaction until multiplied by the axle magnitude.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nTest endpoints: RA(0)=1, RA(L)=0, and RA+RB=1 at every intermediate position.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nCompare how a weighbridge axle position changes load sharing between two bearings. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: The ordinate is a reaction per unit moving load, not a real reaction until multiplied by the axle magnitude. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Compare how a weighbridge axle position changes load sharing between two bearings.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
