          "id": "sec-6-6",
          "secNumber": "6.6",
          "heading": "Two-axle train on a bridge",
          "content": "### 6.3 Two-axle train on a bridge\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nA moving vehicle changes the load positions in a fixed coordinate system. Reactions are calculated from moment equilibrium using only axles currently on the structure.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**RB=\u03a3(P_i x_i/L), RA=\u03a3 P_i\u2212RB, and M(s)=RA s\u2212\u03a3_{x_i\u2264s}P_i(s\u2212x_i).**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nFor a 20 m span carrying 80 kN at x=9 m and 110 kN at x=5 m, RB=(80\u00d79+110\u00d75)/20=63.5 kN and RA=126.5 kN.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nIncluding an axle after it has left the bridge makes both reaction and moment equilibrium wrong; the load entry and exit cases must be explicit.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nUse Lab 10 to move the front axle across the bridge and check reaction sums when only one axle lies on the span.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nInvestigate the difference between maximum midspan moment and maximum shear near a support. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Including an axle after it has left the bridge makes both reaction and moment equilibrium wrong; the load entry and exit cases must be explicit. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Investigate the difference between maximum midspan moment and maximum shear near a support.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
