          "heading": "Envelopes and spacing effects",
          "content": "### 6.4 Envelopes and spacing effects\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nA response envelope is the extreme over all permitted positions and load configurations, not a diagram for one arbitrary vehicle position. Closely spaced axles may create higher local moments.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**For a set of axle positions {x_i(a)}, M_max(s)=max_a M(s;a), subject to the placement constraints.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nAn axle train with two heavy close-spaced loads may govern a near-midspan section even if the heaviest individual axle alone governs a different section.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nOptimizing only the load position but not wheel spacing is insufficient when multiple vehicle configurations are possible.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nCompute an envelope on a grid of positions, then refine around peaks to avoid grid-discretization bias.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nDescribe the role of lane distribution and dynamic allowance in a real bridge loading specification. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Optimizing only the load position but not wheel spacing is insufficient when multiple vehicle configurations are possible. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Describe the role of lane distribution and dynamic allowance in a real bridge loading specification.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
