          "id": "sec-6-8",
          "secNumber": "6.8",
          "heading": "Continuous-span moving-load model",
          "content": "### 6.5 Continuous-span moving-load model\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nOn a continuous beam a moving axle can influence negative support moments as well as positive span moments. Stiffness analysis or closed-form influence surfaces may be required.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**For a loaded beam element, a point force at local coordinate \u03be contributes f_e=P [N1(\u03be),N2(\u03be),N3(\u03be),N4(\u03be)] with cubic Hermite shape functions.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nMoving a load from the center of the left span to the center of the right span can reverse the relative contribution to the interior support moment of a two-span bridge.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nPlacing a point force on whichever mesh node happens to be nearest introduces an artificial force-position jump and distorts influence lines.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nRepeat with refined elements and test convergence of the support-moment response at the same physical axle locations.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nOutline how a highway bridge software verification model can use a simple-span influence line as a baseline. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Placing a point force on whichever mesh node happens to be nearest introduces an artificial force-position jump and distorts influence lines. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Outline how a highway bridge software verification model can use a simple-span influence line as a baseline.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
