          "content": "### 8.4 Verification versus validation\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nVerification asks whether the equations are solved correctly. Validation asks whether the selected equations and assumptions describe the physical system sufficiently for the intended decision. Both are needed.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**A useful error metric is \u03b5_rel=|Q_num\u2212Q_ref|/max(|Q_ref|,Q_floor), with a declared floor to avoid division by near-zero reference values.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nA numerical model can pass a reaction-balance check perfectly while still be invalid for a deep beam if shear deformation is neglected.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nReporting a visually smooth graph as evidence of physical correctness confuses graphical polish with conservation laws and governing mechanics.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nUse manufactured solutions, independent benchmarks, limiting cases, unit checks and browser interaction tests as separate items in the audit trail.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nCreate a model-review sheet comparing a hand calculation, an independent solver and experimental observations. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Reporting a visually smooth graph as evidence of physical correctness confuses graphical polish with conservation laws and governing mechanics. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Create a model-review sheet comparing a hand calculation, an independent solver and experimental observations.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
