          "id": "sec-8-8",
          "secNumber": "8.8",
          "heading": "Ethical reporting and engineering decisions",
          "content": "### 8.5 Ethical reporting and engineering decisions\n\n#### Learning target and physical interpretation\n\nEngineering reports should separate educational demonstrations from design deliverables. Inputs, output units, coordinate conventions, load factors, code versions and unresolved limitations belong alongside figures.\n\n#### Governing relation\n\n**Factor-of-safety or utilization ratios are meaningful only when the load and resistance bases are compatible and the applicable design methodology is explicit.**\n\n#### Worked engineering interpretation\n\nA 10 mm educational deflection model may teach mechanics, but does not establish compliance with serviceability limits unless span limits, support conditions, load combinations and code clauses are checked.\n\n#### What the idealization omits\n\nPresenting an unvalidated lab as a certified design engine can expose users to unsafe decisions despite correct algebra in selected demonstrations.\n\n#### Verification and interpretation of results\n\nList what must be independently checked before a model could inform real structural assessment, including site measurements and licensed engineering review.\n\n#### Applied case and decision boundary\n\nCompose a concise peer-review request that includes model files, assumptions, test evidence and open issues. Before drawing a design conclusion, identify which operating condition governs and which variation in span, load, support restraint or stiffness most affects the answer. If the calculation is to be used beyond teaching, a competent engineer must apply the relevant local code, material specification, site conditions, partial factors and independent review. This book's interactive demonstrations do not implement those obligations. Use the result to formulate better questions about the structure, not to replace a complete engineering assessment.\n\n#### Practice and reflection\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated model, identify the equilibrium equation and the additional compatibility or constitutive equation required. Rewrite the derivation with explicit coordinate signs.\n2. **Numerical perturbation:** Repeat the worked interpretation after increasing the principal load parameter by 10%, keeping the other inputs fixed. Predict the response before calculating it and state whether proportionality is theoretically expected.\n3. **Boundary-condition test:** Replace one ideal support or restraint with a more flexible or more restrictive condition. Explain qualitatively which actions and movements are likely to change and what additional data are required.\n4. **Error diagnosis:** Construct a plausible incorrect solution based on the warning in this lesson: Presenting an unvalidated lab as a certified design engine can expose users to unsafe decisions despite correct algebra in selected demonstrations. Explain which physical check exposes the error.\n5. **Professional judgment:** Using the case **Compose a concise peer-review request that includes model files, assumptions, test evidence and open issues.**, identify one observable site measurement that would be needed to validate the idealization and one design-code question the classroom model cannot answer.\n\n**Model-check prompt:** State in one sentence what would falsify the predicted response. State separately what numerical residual could be tested without any field data."
